@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Device-sync dirty-payload credential classification
+
+Status: in_progress
+Created: 2026-08-09
+Updated: 2026-08-09
+
+## Goal
+
+- Remove encrypted payload hydration and provider-aware classification from the
+  connection-epoch supersession transaction by persisting the server-owned
+  credential-independence decision when each dirty payload is accepted.
+
+## Success criteria
+
+- Every new `device_sync_dirty_payload` row carries the classification derived
+  by the existing generic and Junction-specific import-authority predicates.
+- Classification, compression, KMS-backed key preparation, and encryption
+  finish before the health-data admission transaction begins.
+- Ordinary dirty writes retain the existing revision compare-and-swap, while
+  companion receipt claims, acknowledgement, reconnect supersession, and
+  account deletion retain their reviewed marker-first ordering. No network,
+  KMS, dynamic import, or payload-dependent loop runs under that lock.
+- Connection replacement classifies nullable rows written by an older deployed
+  Web build before the short supersession commit and fails retryably if deploy
+  skew creates another nullable row before the marker is locked.
+- Reconnect retains credential-independent payloads and deletes
+  credential-scoped payloads with set-based database mutations.
+- Focused unit, real PostgreSQL concurrency, schema/migration, typecheck, lint,
+  exact-head CI, and required review gates pass before handoff.
+
+## Constraints
+
+- Reuse the existing credential-independence predicates; do not create a second
+  provider policy or infer authority from ciphertext.
+- Keep the migration additive and compatible with old writers by leaving the
+  new column nullable during the expand window.
+- Do not add a queue, scheduler, repair table, background classifier, or second
+  dirty-state owner.
+- Preserve encrypted payload contents, deterministic payload ids, replay
+  behavior, mailbox handoff, and runtime acknowledgement semantics.
+
+## Tasks
+
+1. Add the nullable persisted classification and migration contract.
+2. Move payload classification and sealing into an explicit pre-transaction
+   preparation phase used by every hosted admission caller.
+3. Replace reconnect hydration with bounded legacy preparation plus set-based
+   supersession under the dirty-marker lock.
+4. Prove replay, contention, account-deletion ordering, migration compatibility,
+   and absence of crypto/provider work inside the supersession transaction.
+5. Reconcile current `main`, run focused verification and exact-head CI, resolve
+   all review findings, and leave the PR unmerged.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- New payloads persist the existing server-derived credential-independence
+  decision beside their ciphertext; no health value or provider secret is
+  exposed.
+- Payload classification, compression, and secure-box sealing now happen before
+  admission opens a transaction. Ordinary writes retain their revision CAS;
+  companion receipt claims retain marker-first ordering only where required.
+- Reconnect classifies old nullable rows outside the transaction and performs
+  marker reset plus credential-scoped deletion without decrypting payloads or
+  loading Junction code under the lock.
+- The additive schema remains compatible with old deployed writers. A future
+  contract migration may make the column non-null only after the writer floor
+  and production null census prove it safe.
+- Verification and review results are recorded in the pull-request description.
